<h2>What the magistrate did</h2>
<p>The aggrieved women sought recovery of four months of unpaid maintenance — Rs 36,000 — through the enforcement route of Section 28 of the DV Act. The magistrate closed the proceedings on the footing that the respondent had already undergone his sentence for the default. On that logic, the jail term worked like payment: once served, nothing remained to recover.</p>

<h2>The argument the High Court will now hear</h2>
<p>Counsel for the revisionists put the point in a sentence the order records: &ldquo;sentencing a person to imprisonment is a mode of enforcement and not a mode of satisfaction of the liability&rdquo; (para 3), relying on <em>Smt. Kuldip Kaur v. Kuldeep Singh</em>, AIR 1989 SC 232. In that decision, rendered under Section 125(3) CrPC, the Supreme Court drew the distinction that governs this field: imprisonment of a maintenance defaulter is coercive machinery to compel payment, and the amount remains recoverable after release. The High Court found the matter deserving of deliberation (para 6), admitted the revision and issued notice.</p>
<div class="note">
<p><strong>To be precise about the stage:</strong> the High Court has decided nothing on merits yet. The significance of the order lies in what it signals — that a closure of recovery proceedings on the &ldquo;sentence undergone&rdquo; theory is sufficiently doubtful to warrant revisional scrutiny.</p>
</div>

<h2>The enforcement architecture for DV Act money orders</h2>
<div class="flow">
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Order:</strong> Monetary relief under Section 20 or interim relief under Section 23 of the DV Act fixes the monthly obligation.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Direct-payment direction:</strong> Section 20(6) permits the magistrate to order the respondent&rsquo;s employer or debtor to pay the aggrieved person directly from salary or dues.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Recovery:</strong> Through Section 28, the machinery of the criminal procedure code applies — warrants for levy of the amount due, attachment of movables, and recovery as if a fine.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Coercion:</strong> For continuing default, imprisonment may follow — month by month — but as pressure to pay, never as a discharge of the debt.</div>
</div>

<h2>Why the distinction matters</h2>
<p>If serving a short default sentence wiped the slate clean, every recalcitrant payer would face a simple arithmetic: a few weeks in custody is cheaper than years of maintenance. The <em>Kuldip Kaur</em> rule closes that escape. It aligns the DV Act and Section 144 BNSS regimes with their protective purpose — the orders exist to sustain dependants, and only money, not martyrdom, sustains anyone. The same principle underpins the practice of successive warrants and fresh sentences for fresh defaults, and of treating arrears as a continuing civil debt recoverable even through separate execution routes.</p>

<h2>Practical guidance</h2>
<div class="check">
<ul>
  <li>For aggrieved persons: when a defaulter emerges from a default sentence, renew the recovery application — press for warrants of attachment and, where the respondent is employed, a Section 20(6) direct-payment direction.</li>
  <li>Keep an arrears ledger before the court: period-wise computation, payments received, and the balance. Recovery proceedings move on arithmetic.</li>
  <li>For respondents: the only safe harbour is payment or a judicially sanctioned modification under Section 25 of the DV Act on proof of changed circumstances — not the default sentence.</li>
  <li>Watch this revision: a reasoned High Court ruling will give Uttarakhand&rsquo;s magistracy — and persuasive guidance beyond — a clear answer on the &ldquo;sentence undergone&rdquo; fallacy.</li>
</ul>
</div>
